      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Troponin-T is a sensitive indicator of minor myocardial damage during coronary bypass surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Troponin-T levels were assessed before and repeatedly for 64 hours after coronary bypass surgery in 100 patients with unstable and 100 with stable angina pectoris."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Postoperative troponin-T levels rose significantly within 6 hours followed by a decline until 64 hours. In unstable patients who had pain within two days and an acute myocardial infarction within two weeks before the operation (Braunwald class IIIC), Troponin-T rose to high levels that persisted for 64 h. Sixty-seven percent of these patients had an elevated troponin-T >0.10 microg/l already before the operation and a perioperative myocardial infarction was recorded in 27%. In contrast, postoperative troponin-T levels in remaining patients classified as unstable were similar to those in patients with stable angina. Elective operations in the control group were performed with a low risk of adverse postoperative events."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Patients with Braunwald class IIIC unstable angina suffered to a great extent myocardial cell damage following coronary bypass surgery."
        }
      ]},
